1582:
(a) Purpose. The purpose of this chapter is to protect, preserve and enhance the rural and wooded character of the city through tree protection and landscaping; to establish protective regulations for trees in the city and to better control problems of flooding, soil erosion, air pollution, heat gain and noise in the city and to make the city a more attractive, healthier and safer place in which to live. This chapter is intended to further the city's goal that all development sites, where trees are most commonly removed, will achieve upon project completion a uniform standard in connection with tree coverage.
(b) Findings. Trees are a valuable asset to the rural/urban environment of the city and generate such benefits as:
(1) The purification of air;
(2) The moderation of the micro-climate;
(3) The reduction of noise and glare;
(4) The conservation of energy in terms of heating and cooling;
(5) The prevention of soil erosion;
(6) Reduced costs in terms of stormwater management;
(7) The minimization of flood potential;
(8) Improved water quality;
(9) The enhancement and stabilization of property values;
(10) Increased aesthetics; and
(11) The preservation of rural character.
(c) Intent. The intent of this chapter is to encourage the protection of trees within the city which the city stands to lose unless protective measures are taken. The intent is not punitive, or to cause hardship to any individual or governmental agency, or private or public company, who uses every care and diligence to protect trees within the city; nor is it meant to inflate the cost of development over a reasonable value of the trees involved.
(Code 1966, § 24A-2; Ord. No. 92-06, § 1, 8-10-1992; Ord. No. 2012-15, § 1, 12-10-2012)

