(a) Except as otherwise provided herein, the terms and provisions of this chapter shall apply to any activity on real property which involves the removal of trees greater than six inches DBH or requires the issuance of a land disturbance permit. This chapter shall not apply to the following:
(1) Property containing dead, diseased or infested trees as determined by the county extension service or state forestry commission;
(2) Orchards or tree nurseries in active commercial operation;
(3) Property involved in a viable agricultural operation (establishment, cultivation or harvesting of fields), or livestock operation;
(4) Cemeteries;
(5) Golf courses; or
(6) Grandfathered projects.
(b) This chapter shall not apply to any portion of a property included within the limits of a valid land disturbance permit issued prior to the effective date of this chapter, provided that all time constraints relating to the permit issued shall be observed.
(c) The requirements of this chapter may be waived by the city administrator for a land disturbance permit which is to proceed with development of a larger project of which at least 75 percent of the land area has already received a permit or permits initiating clearing or grading activities prior to the effective date of the ordinance from which this section is derived.
(d) In no event shall any grandfathered project be extended for a greater time period than 24 months from the date of enactment of the ordinance from which this section is derived.
(Code 1966, § 24A-4; Ord. No. 92-06, § 1, 8-10-1992; Ord. No. 2012-15, § 1, 12-10-2012)

1590:
Prior to the removal of any tree or the issuance of a land disturbance permit, a written application for a tree removal permit and issuance of the same by the city administrator is required of those persons or entities to which this chapter is applicable. The application for a tree removal permit shall include the following:
(1) A tree protection plan (TPP) shall be submitted to the city administrator or his designee for review to ascertain conformance to this chapter along with the application for a land disturbance permit. The TPP may be combined with required landscape plan, construction plans or site plans when possible. The TPP shall provide sufficient information and detail to clearly demonstrate that all applicable requirements and standards of this chapter will be fully satisfied. The TPP must be prepared by a qualified landscape architect, urban forester or arborist except when the land disturbance permit is for the construction of a single detached single-family residence. In the case of a detached single-family residence, the TPP may be prepared by the owner or contractor applying for the land disturbance permit. Homeowners desiring to remove trees on a developed residential lot of record shall not be required to provide a TPP with their application.
The TPP shall show the following information:
a. The project name, land district, land lot, and parcel number, north arrow and scale (no greater than 1" = 100');
b. The property owner's name address and telephone number;
c. The developer's name, address, and telephone number;
d. The name, address, and telephone number of the professional landscape architect, urban forester, or arborist responsible for preparation of the plan, and the seal or statement of professional qualifications of said person;
e. Legal description of the property with the total acreage of the site and total acreage exclusive of all zoning buffer areas and floodplain;
f. Delineation of all areas located within a 100-year floodplain;
g. The boundaries of all required buffer areas;
h. The boundaries and description of all land disturbing activities;
i. Existing natural features and topography at two-foot contour intervals. (In areas where slopes are 20 percent or above, a five-foot contour interval may be used.)
j. All proposed topographic changes;
k. All existing and proposed improvements (structures, buildings, parking areas, stormwater management facilities, streets, etc.);
l. All existing and proposed overhead and below ground utilities and easements.
m. The location of all existing trees and specimen trees which will be retained to fulfill density requirements and their size, dripline area and species (common name). Five or more trees the dripline of which combine into one tree protection area may be outlined as a group and their number, size and species listed in a summary table. All tree protection areas are to be outlined and labeled;
n. The location of all trees to be removed (removal of trees in floodplain shall not be permitted);
o. Specifications for the removal of existing trees and tree preservation techniques and maintenance methods to be employed with existing trees during construction;
p. A summary table of the number of existing trees to remain and new trees to be planted shall be shown along with calculations showing the tree density achieved for the site. Additional credits shall be noted where applicable. Groupings of trees in tree protection areas and areas for new tree planting may be keyed to the summary table by area rather than having each tree individually labeled on the plan;
q. Grade changes or other work adjacent to a tree which would affect it adversely, with specifications on how the grade, drainage and aeration will be maintained around the tree;
(2) A landscape plan shall be submitted supplemental to the TPP and shall show the location and name of all existing and new trees and plants to be retained and/or planted to fulfill density and landscape requirements. The landscape plan shall be shall be prepared by a state registered landscape architect. The landscape plan shall show the following:
a. The project name, land district, land lot, and parcel number, north arrow and scale (no greater than 1" = 50');
b. The property owner's name address and telephone number;
c. The developer's name, address, and telephone number;
d. The name, address, and telephone number and seal of the professional landscape architect;
e. Legal description of the property with the total acreage of the site and total acreage exclusive of all zoning buffer areas and floodplain;
f. Delineation of all areas located within a 100-year floodplain;
g. The location of all required buffer, tree save and landscape areas;
h. The boundaries of all land disturbing activities;
i. Existing natural features and topography at two-foot contour intervals. (In areas where slopes are 20 percent or above, a five-foot contour interval may be used.)
j. All proposed topographic changes;
k. All existing and proposed improvements (structures, buildings, parking areas, stormwater management facilities, streets, etc.);
l. All existing and proposed overhead and below ground utilities and easements;
m. Density calculations;
n. The common and scientific name, quantity, size and spacing of all proposed new landscaping;
o. The proposed landscaping treatment for all areas disturbed by the tree removal or land disturbance activity;
(3) No land disturbance permit shall be issued until the TPP, landscape plan and application has been reviewed and approved, in writing, by the city administrator or his designee.
(4) The city administrator or his designee shall review the TPP, landscape plan and application and either approve, deny, or disapprove with comments denoting required changes to the TPP or landscape plan within 15 working days of submittal. Upon approval of the TPP and landscape plan, a tree removal permit shall be issued to the applicant. If no action is taken within 15 working days of the submission of the TPP, landscape plan and application, it shall be considered approved and the tree removal permit considered issued by the city administrator or his designee. In the event the TPP, landscape plan or application is denied, the reasons therefore shall be reported, in writing, to the applicant.
(6) The fee for a tree removal permit shall be established by the city and shall be paid at the time of application for the permit and said fee shall be nonrefundable. Said fee shall be paid each time a TPP, landscape plan or application is submitted or resubmitted for review.
(7) The city administrator or his designee shall conduct a final inspection of the property for compliance with the TPP and landscape plan prior to the granting of a certificate of occupancy. The city administrator or his representatives shall have the authority to revoke, suspend or void any land disturbance permit, stop all work on a site or any portion thereof, or withhold a certificate of occupancy in the event there has been a violation of any of the provisions of this chapter.
(8) Any applicant for a tree removal permit who is aggrieved by any decision of the city administrator or his designee relating to the application of this chapter shall have the right to appeal said decision as provided under chapter 80, article VIII, pertaining to the board of zoning appeals.
(9) Any revisions to the proposed development of a property, and any changes reflected in a subsequently submitted tree removal permit application, shall be shown on a revised tree protection plan and landscape plan and be considered as a part of the new or revised tree removal permit prior to the commencement of such changed activities.
(Code 1966, § 24A-8; Ord. No. 92-06, § 1, 8-10-1992; Ord. No. 2012-15, § 1, 12-10-2012)

