As a minimum, the following information and materials should be provided as part of the concept plat application package:
(1) One copy of an accurate and up-to-date survey of the property proposed for subdivision;
(2) One copy of a vicinity map at a scale of 1:600 showing the property in relation to the general area of the city in which it is located;
(3) One copy of a completed project data sheet;
(4) Ten copies of a one-page schematic plat drawn clearly and accurately by a design professional at a scale appropriate for the nature of the project, to include:
a. Scale, north arrow, and date of preparation;
b. Approximate topography;
c. Significant natural features on and adjacent to the property, including wetlands and 100-year floodplain, if appropriate;
d. Significant existing manmade features on the property;
e. Proposed streets, lots, public areas, open spaces, greenbelts, buffers, tree save areas, amenity areas, and other significant proposed improvements;
f. Zoning setbacks, and the approximate area of each lot;
g. Any other features that will be important in the design and development of the project; and
h. Any off-site improvements that may be needed to properly develop the property;
(5) One copy of a reduced 11-inch by 17-inch copy of the concept plat suitable for distribution to the planning commission, the mayor and council, and the general public;
(6) Typical floor plans and elevations of the houses that are planned if a proposed residential subdivision; and
(7) A concept plat review fee.
(Ord. No. 2005-4(1), § 5.2, 2-28-2005; Ord. No. 2012-15, § 1, 12-10-2012)

